{
  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component makes a solid attempt at an accessible select dropdown pattern by utilizing native `<button>` elements, managing focus restoration on Escape/selection, and implementing keyboard arrow controls. However, it suffers from critical ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) pattern gaps: the active option is never communicated to screen reader users during arrow-key navigation (missing `aria-activedescendant` or roving `tabindex`), `<label htmlFor>` cannot associate with a `<button>` element, and options have no stable unique `id`s for assistive technology tracking.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\n- **Prediction**: Missing active descendant communication or roving tabindex so screen reader users don't hear selection changes as they arrow through options. *(Confirmed)*\n- **Prediction**: Incorrect label association (`<label htmlFor=\"...\">` pointing to a non-form control button). *(Confirmed)*\n- **Prediction**: Missing Tab key handling while open, allowing focus to escape into the page without closing or restoring state. *(Confirmed)*\n- **Prediction**: Missing type-ahead or Home/End keyboard shortcuts expected for listboxes. *(Confirmed)*\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (blocks access)\n\n1. **Active option not communicated to screen reader users during arrow-key navigation**\n   - **Location**: `DropdownSelect.jsx:62-73` (Ul container focus and arrow keys)\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value) & WAI-ARIA APG Listbox Pattern\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: Focus is placed on `<ul role=\"listbox\" tabIndex=\"0\">` upon opening. When the user presses ArrowUp or ArrowDown, `selectedIndex` updates in React state and CSS updates visually (`.selected`), but the virtual screen reader cursor remains statically on the parent `<ul>`. Because the `<ul>` lacks `aria-activedescendant=\"option-{selectedIndex}\"` (and individual `<li>` options do not receive programmatic focus), screen reader users hear no spoken feedback when moving through options.\n   - **Fix**: Assign unique IDs to each option (e.g., `id={`dropdown-opt-${index}`}`). On the `ul[role=\"listbox\"]`, add `aria-activedescendant={`dropdown-opt-${selectedIndex}`}`.\n\n2. **Broken programmatic label association (`htmlFor` targeting `<button>`)**\n   - **Location**: `DropdownSelect.jsx:53-56` (`<label htmlFor=\"dropdown-button\">{label}</label>`)\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader and voice-control users\n   - **WCAG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: In HTML specification, the `<label>` element's `for` attribute is only valid for labelable elements (`<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>`, `<meter>`, `<output>`, `<progress>`). Browsers and assistive technologies do not reliably associate a `<label>` with a `<button>`. Clicking the label will not reliably activate the button, and screen readers will announce the button's name solely from its text contents (`{options[selectedIndex]} \u25bc`), omitting the category label entirely.\n   - **Fix**: Assign an `id` to the label (e.g., `id=\"dropdown-label\"`) and associate it to the button using `aria-labelledby=\"dropdown-label dropdown-button\"`.\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n1. **Tab key not handled when listbox is open (unmanaged focus blur)**\n   - **Location**: `DropdownSelect.jsx:31-50` (`handleKeyDown`)\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Keyboard-only and screen reader users\n   - **WCAG Citation**: WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order), WAI-ARIA APG Select-Only Combobox / Listbox\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Why this matters**: If a keyboard user presses `Tab` while the listbox is open, focus tabs out of the listbox to the next focusable page element, but `isOpen` remains `true`. The popover remains floating open over page content, resulting in an orphaned open menu and unexpected focus jumping.\n   - **Fix**: Add a `Tab` key handler in `handleKeyDown`: either commit the selected option and close the dropdown, or dismiss the dropdown and allow focus to advance naturally. Also implement an outside-click / `onBlur` listener to dismiss when focus leaves the component.\n\n2. **Redundant accessible name noise on trigger button (\"\u25bc\")**\n   - **Location**: `DropdownSelect.jsx:60` (`<span aria-hidden=\"true\">\u25bc</span>`)\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users\n   - **WCAG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\n   - **Confidence**: MEDIUM\n   - **Why this matters**: While `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">` correctly hides the glyph, because the button text contains `{options[selectedIndex]}`, some screen reader/browser combinations compute accessible names including whitespaces/line breaks before child nodes. More importantly, when the dropdown is closed, the button communicates the *currently chosen value* rather than the control's purpose unless `aria-labelledby` binds the label.\n   - **Fix**: Ensure `aria-labelledby=\"dropdown-label dropdown-button\"` is present so the announcement follows: *\"[Label], [Current Value], combo box / menu button collapsed\"*.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n1. **Missing `Home`, `End`, and `PageUp`/`PageDown` navigation keys**\n   - **Location**: `DropdownSelect.jsx:31-50`\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WAI-ARIA APG Listbox Keyboard Interaction\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Keyboard-only users\n   - **Fix**: In `handleKeyDown`, bind `Home` to index `0` and `End` to `options.length - 1`.\n\n2. **Incomplete list item keyboard handlers**\n   - **Location**: `DropdownSelect.jsx:75-81` (`<li onKeyDown=... >`)\n   - **Impacted User Group**: Keyboard-only users\n   - **Why this matters**: The `<li>` elements have `onKeyDown` listeners, but since `tabIndex` is not on the items and focus is maintained on the `<ul>`, these `li.onKeyDown` handlers are dead code.\n   - **Fix**: Consolidate keyboard interaction entirely on the container receiving focus (`listRef.current`).\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (best practice not met)\n\n- **Type-ahead search**: Supporting character keys (typing \"C\" jumps to the first option starting with \"C\") is recommended by APG for custom select widgets with longer lists.\n- **Scroll into view**: If the options list exceeds `max-height: 200px` (`overflow-y: auto`), changing `selectedIndex` via arrow keys does not automatically call `.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' })`, leaving selected items off-screen.\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Missing `aria-activedescendant` causes silent arrow navigation; invalid `htmlFor` on button. |\n| Keyboard-only | **MEDIUM** | Missing `Tab` dismissal/commit and lack of `Home`/`End` keys. |\n| Low vision | **LOW** | Outline and contrast styles (`#0066cc`) meet WCAG 2.4.7 focus indicator requirements. |\n| Cognitive | **LOW** | Clear visual styling, straightforward interaction flow. |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No disruptive animations or auto-motion present. |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No media elements or audio output involved. |\n| Environmental contrast | **LOW** | Focus outlines are 3px solid with adequate contrast. |\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing\n\n- **`aria-activedescendant` linkage**: The list container (`role=\"listbox\"`) does not declare which child item is currently active.\n- **Stable IDs for options**: Options lack `id` attributes required for `aria-activedescendant` referencing.\n- **Outside click / blur dismissal**: Clicking away or tabbing out leaves the listbox floating open in DOM.\n- **Auto-scroll synchronization**: When arrowing through overflowing lists, the active option is not brought into visual view.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user**: You will be able to open the dropdown and hear that a listbox has opened. However, as you press down/up arrows, you will hear complete silence because focus remains on the parent list without `aria-activedescendant`. You will not know which item is highlighted until you select it and focus returns to the button.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Navigating with arrows and Escape works well on the happy path. However, pressing `Tab` while open causes focus to abruptly escape to page links/inputs behind the dropdown, leaving the menu stuck open.\n- **Low vision user**: High-contrast outline (`outline: 3px solid #0066cc`) is clearly visible. The `.dropdown-list` scroll container needs programmatic scroll-into-view so keyboard focus doesn't select items hidden outside the `200px` scroll window.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\n**REVISE**: While the basic structural skeleton and focus restoration mechanics (returning focus to the trigger on Escape and selection) are implemented well, the component cannot be marked `ACCEPT` because it fails WCAG 4.1.2 and APG Listbox requirements during its primary task: an assistive technology user cannot tell what option is highlighted while arrowing through the list.\n\n**Requirements to Upgrade to ACCEPT:**\n1. Implement `aria-activedescendant` on the `ul[role=\"listbox\"]` referencing `id`s on the child `li[role=\"option\"]`.\n2. Replace `htmlFor=\"dropdown-button\"` with `aria-labelledby=\"dropdown-label dropdown-button\"`.\n3. Handle `Tab` and outside-blur events to close the menu gracefully.\n",
